Key observation: `await opened.timeout(_sheetSettleGrace)` — when `opened.timeout` times out, the `opened` future itself is NOT cancelled (Dart futures can't be cancelled). If `opened` later completes with an error, that error becomes an unhandled async error! Let me check: `opened` is `Future<void>`. `opened.timeout(...)` creates a new future; if timeout fires, the new future completes with TimeoutException, but `opened` remains pending. If `opened` later completes with an error, that error is unhandled → in Flutter that crashes the app (FlutterError.reportError / unhandled async error zone) — actually in Dart, an unhandled error on a Future without a listener triggers an unhandled exception in the current Zone. In Flutter, that would be reported to FlutterError... Actually for futures, an unhandled error is reported via the Zone's unhandled error handler, which in Flutter prints and can crash in tests.

Wait, but before this change, the code did `await opened;` unconditionally — so `opened` always had a listener. Now, when the timeout fires, there's still a listener attached via `.timeout()` — does `.timeout()` keep listening to the original future? Yes! `Future.timeout` internally listens to `this` future: it registers `then` on the source future to forward the result to the result completer. Let me check the Dart SDK implementation of `Future.timeout`:

```dart
Future<T> timeout(Duration timeLimit, {FutureOr<T> Function()? onTimeout}) {
    ...
    _Future<T> _future = _Future<T>();
    Timer timer;
    ...
    timer = Timer(timeLimit, () {
      ...
      if (onTimeout == null) {
        _future._completeError(TimeoutException("Future not completed", timeLimit));
      } ...
    });
    this.then((T v) {
      if (timer.isActive) {
        timer.cancel();
        _future._completeWithValue(v);
      }
    }, onError: (Object e, StackTrace s) {
      if (timer.isActive) {
        timer.cancel();
        _future._completeError(e, s);
      }
    });
    return _future;
  }
```

So `this.then(...)` attaches both a value and an error handler to `opened`. After the timeout fires, `timer.isActive` is false, so the handlers do nothing — but crucially they ARE handlers, so the error on `opened` is considered handled (the `then` with onError swallows it). So no unhandled error. Good — `.timeout()` attaches persistent listeners, so there's no unhandled async error when the sheet eventually settles after the grace.

